"""Source-grounded teaching for Lecture18, with no Lecture18 classroom transcript."""
TITLE='Neuron, Neural Networks, LLMs and Agents'
REUSE={}
PAGES={1: ('Neuron, neural networks, LLMs and agents', 'title', [], []), 2: ('From a neuron to agents that communicate', 'concept', ['**A neuron takes a vector and returns one number.** Each input is multiplied by its weight, and the products are added to give the output. This weighted sum is an **inner product**. A neural network joins many such operations into layers, so one input vector becomes a **decision vector**, with a response for each possible answer.', '**A large language model takes many vectors and predicts what comes next.** Fifteen token vectors pass through five layers in the example. Each layer changes their representations through its weights, and the final output is the sixteenth vector, a **probability distribution** over the next token. The values express which continuations the model treats as more or less likely.', '**An agentic system connects several LLMs into a graph.** The Outline model develops a plan, the Write model produces a draft, the Critic model evaluates it, the Revise model changes it, and the Final model produces the answer. The connection between Critic and Revise forms a loop, allowing a revision to be evaluated again.', 'The agents communicate through **language**. One model’s written output becomes information another model can use, so the graph coordinates whole language models rather than individual neurons. The agents can speak to each other in English, while the models can also work in other human languages and computer languages.', '**Inference uses inner products, and learning uses SGD with dynamic programming.** Inference processes the current inputs with the learned weights. **Stochastic gradient descent**, or SGD, changes weights to reduce prediction error, while backpropagation uses **dynamic programming** to organize the backward calculation. These operations support the small network as well as the language models joined into the agentic graph.'], ['inner product', 'decision vector', 'next-token probability', 'agent graph', 'critic and revision loop', 'human and computer languages', 'inference versus learning']), 3: ('Parallel heads and successive layers', 'concept', ['**Predicting the next word is difficult because the prediction depends on information spread through the sequence.** A language model therefore uses many layers of processing. Each layer receives representations of the input and changes them before passing them onward, giving the following layer more useful information for the prediction.', '**Within a layer, several heads work in parallel.** A head is one of the processing components that contributes to the layer’s representation. The heads are described as different experts because each can contribute a different part of the information the layer uses. They work alongside one another rather than waiting for one head to finish before another begins.', '**The layer brings the heads’ contributions together.** The result is an improved set of representations, written as **x**, for the sequence. A representation is the information carried by a vector about an input item. Combining several contributions lets that representation incorporate more than one head contributes on its own.', '**The next layer repeats the process with the representations it receives.** It has parallel heads whose contributions are combined again. The model therefore develops its representations through a succession of layers, rather than making its final prediction directly from the original input vectors.', '**Heads and layers add different kinds of structure.** Heads provide parallel contributions within one processing stage, while layers provide successive stages that refine the vectors further. The prediction at the end depends on these contributions being combined within stages and developed across stages.'], ['next-word prediction', 'parallel heads', 'heads as experts', 'combined representations', 'successive layers', 'parallel versus successive processing']), 4: ('A dynamical rule across time and depth', 'mechanism', ['**A dynamical system takes a state, applies a rule, and uses the resulting state as input again.** In the oscillator example, the state contains position **x** and velocity **v**. It begins with position one and velocity zero, and repeatedly updating those two quantities produces the changing motion.', '**Position and velocity take turns being updated.** The new position uses the old velocity, and the new velocity then uses that new position. The step size **h** determines how far the calculation advances at each update. Position traces a cosine-like oscillation while velocity follows a shifted oscillation, so a simple repeated rule builds an entire changing trajectory.', '**Jordan’s recurrent network also feeds its output back into its input.** Plan units and state units feed a hidden layer, which produces an output. That output is copied into the state units, and their small self-loops preserve a fading trace of earlier states. The weights are reused at each time step, allowing recent outputs to influence the next output in a sequence of speech movements.', '**In the LLM comparison, depth plays the role of time.** Fifteen vectors enter the first layer and are transformed by its weights. The resulting vectors enter the next layer, where another transformation takes place. Following one number upward through the layers gives a sequence of changing values, which can be drawn as a curve even though it is not the oscillator’s cosine.', '**The rules are organized differently in these systems.** The oscillator and Jordan network apply the same rule repeatedly as time advances. The LLM uses a separate set of weights at each layer, so its successive transformations follow the depth of the network. What connects the examples is the dependence of each new state on the state produced just before it. The small stock-return graph traces Small, Medium and Large series over the same period. They fluctuate, rise and fall over the years, giving another example of quantities changing over time.'], ['state and rule', 'interleaved position and velocity updates', 'feedback', 'Jordan state units', 'reused weights', 'depth as time', 'layer-specific weights']), 5: ('One prediction task for different sequences', 'concept', ['The page starts with **15 vectors, x₁ to x₁₅**, and an empty slot for **x₁₆**, and asks **vectors of what?** An LLM receives a sequence of vectors and predicts the next one. The four reveals show four different kinds of sequence fed into the same arrangement.', 'First, a **sentence cut into 15 words**. “The cat that the dog chased ran up the old tree and sat on the ___.” Each vector stands for one word, and **x₁₆ is the missing word**. Predicting it requires using words far back in the sentence, since it is the cat that sat on something.', 'Next, the words disappear and a **spectrogram is cut into 15 frames**, from 0 to 5 kHz along the vertical axis with time running to the right. Each vector is now one slice of sound, and **x₁₆ is the next frame**. Then an **image of a house, a tree and the sun is cut into 16 patches**, read row by row. Fifteen patches are vectors, and **x₁₆ is the missing bottom-right patch**.', 'The last example is a **protein, the insulin B chain**, read as **15 amino acids**, F V N Q H L C G S H L V E A L, drawn folding into a helix. Each amino acid is a vector, and **x₁₆ is the next residue**, which is **Y**. The same sixteenth slot sits at the end of every sequence.', 'The takeaway states the point of the four examples. **The LLM only ever sees vectors.** Words, sounds, pixels and molecules all become sequences of vectors, so **one machine asks one question, what is x₁₆?** Whatever the source, learning to predict the next vector in a sequence is the task the model is built for.'], ['15 input vectors', 'x₁₆ prediction', 'words, frames, patches, amino acids', 'vectors as a common format']), 6: ('Which sequences can be English words?', 'concept', ['The page asks which strings of sounds can be English words, building the answer in three rows. The first row has twenty **one-syllable words with a single consonant, a single vowel and a single consonant**, such as Don, mat, sit, cup, fish and ring. Consonants are dark, vowels red, and a silent e grey. The rule for this row is that **a simple syllable in English can have a consonant, then a vowel, then a consonant**.', 'The second row adds **clusters**, groups of consonants. Real words with clusters before the vowel, called **onsets**, include split, flat, black, glad and stop. Real words with clusters after the vowel, called **codas**, include desk, box, help, milk and lamp. Each real word has an **impossible twin** below it, marked with a star, such as *sblit, *lfat, *bnack, *dlad and *tsop, and *desb, *bokp, *hepl, *mikl and *lapm.', 'Many twins use the same letters in the opposite order, flat and *lfat, help and *hepl, lamp and *lapm. **Order matters**, and the pattern mirrors around the vowel. An onset like fl is fine, but the same sounds reversed as lf cannot start a word, although the reversed order can end a word. The rule for this row is that **only certain sequences are allowed before the vowel and after the vowel**.', 'The third row has **words of many syllables**, with dots marking syllable breaks, such as **ba·na·na, win·dow, pump·kin, nap·kin, At·lan·tic, ath·lete, ad·mit and spa·ghet·ti**. Atlantic, athlete and admit contain t followed by l, th followed by l, and d followed by m. Those pairs are fine inside the word because a syllable break falls between them, but they cannot begin a word, so *tlantic, *thlete and *dmit are impossible. Likewise ng can end a syllable, as in ring, but never start one, so *ngapkin is impossible.', 'The rule for the whole page is that **a long word is a chain of syllables**, and every syllable obeys the same rules. **t·l is fine across a break, but tl cannot start a syllable.** This is **phonotactic knowledge**, the knowledge of which sound sequences a language allows, and English speakers have it without ever being taught it.'], ['CVC syllables', 'onset and coda clusters', 'impossible twins', 'order mirrors around the vowel', 'syllable breaks', 'phonotactics']), 7: ('Guessing the next letter reveals phonotactic knowledge', 'concept', ['The pop quiz asks how **phonotactic knowledge can be revealed through GPT learning**. Each item is the start of an English word, and the task is to **guess the next letter**. The nine starts are ra_, sp_, k_, skr_, m_, bram_, b_, f_ and sh_. People agree quickly on most of them, and how much they agree changes from item to item.', 'After **ra_**, likely letters include t, n, r and l. **After a vowel, almost anything** can follow, so the guesses spread out. After **sp_**, the next letter is **l, r or a vowel**, and *spn is never possible. After **k_**, a vowel or **n**, as in knee, and never *kt. After **b_** and **f_**, the choices are **r, l or a vowel**, never *bn or *fn. After **sh_**, a vowel or **r**, as in shrimp, never *shb.', 'Some starts narrow the choices even more. After **skr_** only a vowel can come next, never *skrl, and after **m_** at the start of a word only a vowel, never *mb. After **bram_**, the letter b is almost forced, as in bramble, though a new syllable could also start there. At the start of a word the choices shrink sharply, while after a vowel they open up again.', 'These guesses follow the rules for English syllables. What can follow a starting consonant is exactly what an English onset allows, and the impossible continuations are clusters that English never allows at the start of a syllable. Guessing well requires knowing English phonotactics.', 'The takeaway connects this to LLMs. **Guessing the next letter is predicting x₁₆.** A **GPT trained only on this game learns English sounds and spelling**, because its predicted distribution has to match these patterns. After ra_ its distribution is spread broad over many letters, and after skr_ it is sharply peaked on a few vowels.'], ['next-letter prediction', 'constrained versus open continuations', 'impossible continuations', 'next-token prediction', 'GPT learning phonotactics']), 8: ('Design an agentic system for English syllables', 'prompt', [], [])}
ORIGINAL={2: ['A **single neuron** multiplies its inputs by weights and adds the products into one number. A **neural network** combines these operations into layers, turning one input vector into a decision vector.', 'An **LLM** processes fifteen vectors through successive layers and predicts a sixteenth vector, a probability distribution over the next token. An **agentic system** joins whole LLMs into a graph that outlines, writes, criticizes, revises and finalizes the response.', 'The critic and reviser form a loop so a revised draft can be evaluated again. The agents communicate in English and can work in other human languages and computer languages.', '**Inference uses inner products**, while learning uses **SGD and dynamic programming**. Inference processes current inputs with learned weights, while learning changes the weights to reduce error.'], 3: ['**Predicting the next word is difficult**, so an LLM uses many layers to develop its input representations. Each layer transforms the vectors produced by the preceding layer.', 'Within a layer, **heads work in parallel as different experts**. Their contributions are combined into new representations, and the next layer repeats the process with those representations. Heads contribute within one stage, while layers provide successive stages.'], 4: ['A **dynamical system** applies a rule to its state and feeds the resulting state back in. In the oscillator, position is updated using the old velocity, then velocity is updated using that new position. Repeating the rule produces oscillation.', 'In **Jordan’s recurrent network**, plan and state units feed a hidden layer, output returns to state units, and self-connections preserve a fading trace. The weights are reused over time, so recent outputs affect the next output.', 'An **LLM** transforms vectors through successive layers, so depth plays the role of time. Following one number through the layers gives a changing curve. Each layer has its own weights, unlike the same rule reused across time in the oscillator and recurrent network.', 'The small stock-return graph traces Small, Medium and Large series fluctuating over the same period, another example of quantities changing over time.'], 5: ['An **LLM** receives fifteen vectors and predicts the following one. The vectors can represent words in a sentence, frames of a spectrogram, patches of a picture, or amino acids in the **insulin B chain**.', 'The picture is divided into sixteen patches, with fifteen given and the bottom-right patch left to predict. In the protein example the next residue is Y. The original materials differ, but all are represented as **sequences of vectors**.'], 6: ['One-syllable words like Don, mat and fish follow a **consonant, vowel, consonant** pattern. Clusters are allowed **before and after the vowel** only in certain orders, so split, flat, desk and help are words while *sblit, *lfat, *desb and *hepl are impossible, with the order mirroring around the vowel.', 'A **long word is a chain of syllables**, each obeying the same rules. t·l is fine across a break in At·lan·tic, but **tl cannot start a syllable**, so *tlantic is impossible, and ng can end a syllable but never start one.'], 7: ['Guessing the **next letter** of a word start shows **phonotactic knowledge**. After a vowel, as in **ra_**, almost anything can follow. At the start of a word the choices narrow, to **l, r or a vowel** after sp_, b_ and f_, and to **only a vowel** after skr_ and m_.', 'Each item also has an impossible continuation, such as *spn, *kt, *skrl or *mb. **Guessing the next letter is predicting x₁₆**, so a **GPT** trained only on this game learns English sounds and spelling, with a broad distribution after ra_ and a sharply peaked one after skr_.']}
ISSUES={4: ['This deck dates the Jordan network to 1986, while Lecture17 used 1985. The notes prioritize the mechanism and retain the source discrepancy here.', 'An embedded Small/Medium/Large stock-return graph appears near the oscillator. It is described only as time-varying trajectories, because the deck supplies no market mechanism or numerical argument.'], 5: ['The N beside the folded protein marks its amino end, not an answer in the missing slot. The speaker notes identify the next residue as Y.'], 7: ['The visible example is ra_ but the speaker notes discuss fa_. Both illustrate the source’s broad post-vowel choices.']}
